+# Native rotation reservation claim — source contract
+
+This adds rotation_native_claim and rotation_native_release to chitta.rotation/1.
+The record mode is native_v1. It is a bounded reservation stage, not a close,
+restore or receipt acknowledgement API. Host intent/commit/reconciliation is
+still required before the full native transaction is available.
+
+A claim requires a Relay-owned Codex session and the exact latest completed turn,
+including native turn ID, fresh-turn/no-tool observations and both transcript
+watermarks. The request binds a durable host capsule storage receipt and the
+complete source binding to the paired core and foreground reservation receipts.
+The operation/epoch and host claim are distinct UUIDs. Chitta separately issues
+its monotone token and session:sequence claim ID; these identifiers are not
+interchangeable.
+
+Unlike the legacy rotation_claim operation, this explicit native operation does
+not require a later model turn after a rotation signal. It records evidence for
+a caller holding the actual native reservations. JSON receipts, ledger records,
+or replayed status cannot recreate those reservations or authorize a close.
+The frontend and native ordered owner must still revalidate their own capabilities.
+
+The lifetime is at most five seconds and cannot exceed the advertised native
+reservation lifetime. The host must independently enforce the original monotonic
+native deadline before and after the ledger call. Expiry never extends native
+authority. This API has no renewal and never reuses an existing operation ID.
+An uncertain claim response must be looked up by its exact epoch, not resubmitted.
+
+One live claim, of either mode, excludes another claim for the same session.
+Every legacy rotation mutation is refused when targeting a native claim.
+Ordinary completed-turn telemetry remains recordable; a changed turn prevents
+new claims and must invalidate the host's pending native reservation.
+
+Native release requires the exact epoch, issued claim ID/token and host UUID.
+It is idempotent and can close an expired reservation. It cannot release a claim
+from another mode or operation and refuses an existing intent. The future commit
+contract must define sticky accepted intent, receipt validation and recovery
+before any destructive operation is exposed.
+
+All mutations run inside TaskLedger's existing serialized durable transaction.
+WAL persistence failure leaves the prior in-memory state intact. The native
+record survives WAL replay and appears through the existing rotation_status.
+Capsule body bytes remain in the host's private immutable artifact store; this
+ledger record contains their digest and source binding, not model-visible owner
+metadata, credentials, environment or command-line arguments.
